  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Monday...\n\nThis morning will be the coldest morning of the week with low \ntemperatures in the 40s to lower 50s, with a few locations falling\ninto the upper 30s. Temperatures will then warm into the 70s to \nnear 80 degrees under mostly sunny skies today. While winds will \nbe lighter, they will still be breezy out of the northeast over \nmuch of south-central Montana and north-central Wyoming and out of\nthe northwest over far eastern Montana. Dry conditions will also \nexist for most, though there is a low chance (around 15 percent) \nof showers and weak thunderstorms during the afternoon and evening\nover the mountains and foothills of southern Montana and northern\nWyoming, as well as over the plains east of the Bighorn Mountains\nalong the Montana and Wyoming state line. As far as the wildfire \nsmoke goes, hazy skies will continue, with some surface smoke \nexpected to linger over far southern Montana into Wyoming. \n\nWednesday into Thursday will be warmer and dry days as the \ncyclonic flow is replaced by a building ridge of high pressure. \nWith this, look for high temperatures to be in the upper 70s and \n80s Wednesday and upper 80s and 90s Thursday. \n\nWhile hot and dry conditions will likely continue Friday into the\nweekend under the continued influence of the upper level ridge \naloft, there is some uncertainty in the exact temperature forecast\nfor this time. This is due to uncertainty in the timing of a \nslight breakdown of the ridge. Along with impacts to temperature, \nperiods of elevated wind may occur during this time, increasing \nfire weather concerns. \n\nMonday through next week, ensemble forecasts suggest a return of \nmonsoonal moisture and energy over the area. This would favor a \ndaily chance of showers and thunderstorms, potentially beginning \nMonday. While this would bring scattered precipitation back to the\narea, it would also bring lightning, which would increase the \nrisk of lightning caused wildfire starts. LA",
